You're right to flag it — the new comment block is justification-heavy, exactly the pattern your feedback on #1005 called out (43% of its additions were review-facing rationale). Here's what it currently says:

```go
// Dedicated checkpoint_remote URL mode — reported only when push-URL
// derivation actually succeeds, mirroring the pre-push exemption
// (ps.hasCheckpointURL). When derivation fails or falls back (fork/owner
// mismatch, unparseable push-remote URL), the gate applies normal
// single-remote sync on the next push, so status must report that
// instead. PushURL is a local-only computation (git remote get-url,
// settings, URL parsing, repo remotes + env) and is safe for
// network-free status; do NOT call resolvePushSettings here — its
// follow-up metadata fetch does dial. The elected remote is the
// representative probe: a push to a *different* named remote may derive
// differently, which is the (narrow) accepted divergence.
```

Two constraints in there are load-bearing (invisible in the code, regression-prone): the probe must mirror the gate's `hasCheckpointURL` exemption, and status must never call `resolvePushSettings` because its follow-up fetch dials. The rest — the failure-mode enumeration, the inventory of what `PushURL` reads, the "which is the accepted divergence" narration — is rationale that belongs in the commit message (where it already is). Same for the parenthetical added to the fail-closed branch. Trimming both:

